  "text": "To find the first-order shift in the energy of the ground state, we use first-order time-independent perturbation theory.\n\n**1. Identify the unperturbed ground state wavefunction:**\nFor a particle of mass $m$ in a one-dimensional box of width $L$ (from $x=0$ to $x=L$), the unperturbed energy eigenfunctions are:\n$$\\psi_n(x) = \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{L}} \\sin\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)$$\nFor the ground state ($n=1$):\n$$\\psi_1(x) = \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{L}} \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{L}\\right)$$\n\n**2. Apply the first-order energy shift formula:**\nThe first-order correction to the energy $E_1^{(1)}$ is given by the expectation value of the perturbation $V(x)$ in the unperturbed ground state:\n$$E_1^{(1)} = \\langle \\psi_1 | V(x) | \\psi_1 \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{L} \\psi_1^*(x) V(x) \\psi_1(x) \\, dx$$\n\n**3. Substitute the given perturbation and wavefunction:**\nGiven $V(x) = V_0 \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{L}\\right)$, we have:\n$$E_1^{(1)} = \\int_{0}^{L} \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{L}} \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{L}\\right)\\right) \\left(V_0 \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{L}\\right)\\right) \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{L}} \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{L}\\right) \\text{s} \\text{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\text{theorse}\\right)^2}{L} \\textstyle\u043cicros} \\textstyle{L} \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\textstyle{L}\\text{theorsim} \\dots \\text{/text-and {and liveness-and l-tye-tyer-ander-and \\{L-and and linde-and-anderer-andand Leno-and and and the-me((and and L^2-the_emer/L-canet_and  ,, moster-_and \\{and and and and and andand  \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0441-and and and-_and than-the-and and l-the 2-and \\text-and ling-and-sin-and-and-gand-@_, and }\n-and and and and and and and and and and and and and and and and and andandrend-_ander-duty-- indeed and thanandedand and andand and and and andand thanand and}andng-er-and and and and and and and and and and and and and and ander-count-and andthe-the-ander and andsman l-and and and andand and and and and and the and and and and and and